<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Xiaowu of Liu Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">430–464</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Liu Jun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Xiaowu (Liu Jun) was the fourth emperor of the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, reigning from 453 to 464. He came to the throne after defeating his elder brother Liu Shao, who had murdered their father, Emperor Wen. His reign saw lavish building and tighter imperial control over the provincial princes, and the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zu-chongzhi">Zu Chongzhi</a> presented his Daming calendar to him in 462.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Jun was born on 19 September 430, the third son of Emperor Wen by Consort Lu Huinan, who was not among the emperor's favourites; neither was her son. He was created Prince of Wuling in 435 and from the age of nine was rotated through provincial governorships, from 445 holding the key border post of Yong Province. As governor of Xu Province in 450, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taiwu-of-northern-wei">Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei</a> struck south, he insisted on defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> against the wish of his uncle Liu Yigong to abandon it.</p><p>In 453 his elder brother Liu Shao, about to be deposed for hiring a witch to curse their father to death, assassinated Emperor Wen and seized the throne. Liu Jun, then preparing a campaign on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, rose against him with the general Shen Qingzhi, his uncle Liu Yixuan and Zang Zhi. Within a month he neared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and declared himself emperor while the fighting went on; seven days later the palace fell, and Liu Shao was captured and executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 454 Liu Yixuan and Zang Zhi, resentful of the emperor's interference in their provinces and, it was said, of his incest with Liu Yixuan's daughters, rebelled with the governors of Yu and Yan provinces. The rebels were defeated at Liangshan and their leaders killed, and Xiaowu cut the great provinces down in size. He drove his young brother Liu Hun to suicide in 455 over a declaration written in jest, began to curb the princes' authority and relied on low-ranking favourites such as Dai Faxing.</p><p>In 459 his brother Liu Dan rebelled at Guangling. Xiaowu slaughtered the families of Liu Dan's associates, forced his old helper Yan Jun to kill himself on a false charge, and when Shen Qingzhi took the city had most of its people killed. Grief at the death of his favourite concubine Consort Yin in 462 left him unable to govern, and he forced commoners to build her a great tomb and temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Xiaowu died on 12 July 464 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-ziye">Liu Ziye</a>, Emperor Qianfei. His era names were Xiaojian (454-456) and Daming (457-464). He is generally regarded as a capable but harsh and sexually immoral emperor who greatly reduced the powers of officials and princes. Sima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> described him late in his reign as greedy, gambling with returning governors until he had won their wealth, and drunk almost every day, yet so quick to wake to urgent business that his officials feared him and did not dare to be idle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Xiaowu of Song” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Liu Jun</b> rose against him, beheaded him and became Emperor Xiaowu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
